Here's the approach — use an **exception-handling middleware** combined with ASP.NET Core's built-in `ProblemDetails` service:

**In `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Register ProblemDetails services
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails(options =>
{
    options.CustomizeProblemDetails = context =>
    {
        context.ProblemDetails.Extensions["traceId"] = context.HttpContext.TraceIdentifier;
    };
});

var app = builder.Build();

// Global exception handler middleware
app.UseExceptionHandler(exceptionApp =>
{
    exceptionApp.Run(async context =>
    {
        var exception = context.Features.Get<Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics.IExceptionHandlerFeature>()?.Error;

        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Resource not found"),
            ArgumentException => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Invalid request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (StatusCodes.Status500InternalServerError, "An unexpected error occurred")
        };

        context.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;

        var problemDetails = new Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = statusCode == 500 ? null : exception?.Message,
            Instance = context.Request.Path
        };

        await context.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(problemDetails, contentType: "application/problem+json");
    });
});

// Your endpoints
app.MapGet("/items/{id}", (int id) =>
{
    if (id < 0) throw new ArgumentException("ID must be positive");
    if (id == 0) throw new KeyNotFoundException("Item not found");
    return Results.Ok(new { Id = id, Name = "Example" });
});

app.Run();
```

**Key points:**

| Exception | Status | Behavior |
|-----------|--------|----------|
| `KeyNotFoundException` | 404 | Message exposed in `detail` |
| `ArgumentException` | 400 | Message exposed in `detail` |
| `InvalidOperationException` | 409 | Message exposed in `detail` |
| Everything else | 500 | `detail` is `null` (no leak) |

**Response example (404):**
```json
{
  "status": 404,
  "title": "Resource not found",
  "detail": "Item not found",
  "instance": "/items/0",
  "traceId": "00-abc123..."
}
```

This catches all unhandled exceptions globally — no per-endpoint `try-catch` needed. The `UseExceptionHandler` middleware sits at the top of the pipeline and intercepts any exception that bubbles up.
